oods. These individuals could not afford accommodation so grand as an inn, often taking shelter in a farmer’s barn or hayloft.  “High agricultural prices gave farmers the incentive to produce crops for sale in the dearest markets rather than for the satisfaction of rural subsistence. Rising population put intense strain on the markets themselves, especially urban ones: demand for food often outstripped supply. So most urban markets were forced to promulgate stringent regulations whereby local purchasers were given preference over non-residents and outside speculators.”'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2207, 'temperature': 0.2})
